\section*{Uniform finite-metric randomized k-server implementation}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 110; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that finite rational metrics admit a uniform randomized $k$-server implementation with that ratio, polynomial preprocessing and per-request bit cost, and a finite instance-dependent additive allowance.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For integers $n\ge3$ and $2\le k<n$, equip $X=[n]$ with a metric $d$ having rational distances. Choose an initial labeled server tuple $s\in X^k$, allowing repetitions. With full binary input length $L$, the claim supplies one uniform randomized online algorithm, absolute $C$ and a polynomial $p$, whose preprocessing costs at most $p(L)$ and whose response to request $r_t$, on every reachable history and internal state, costs at most
\[
 p(L+\lceil\log_2(t+1)\rceil)
\]
additional bit operations. It uses unbiased bits and moves one selected server to each request, allowing a zero-distance move. For any finite $\sigma$ fixed before the random bits are chosen,
\[
 \mathbb E\operatorname{cost}_{A,s}(\sigma)
 \le C(\log(k+1))^2\operatorname{OPT}_s(\sigma)+B_{d,k,s},
\]
where $\operatorname{OPT}$ is the minimum offline movement cost and $B_{d,k,s}<\infty$ is independent of $\sigma$ and its horizon. The algorithm does not need the horizon in advance.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 110; 2026-09-24), finite rational decision tables realize the existence theorem over a bounded horizon. A capped request filter and fallback service control all created tree entries, while an expanding schedule keeps computation polynomial in the input and binary request count.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 110; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 110; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: finite rational metrics admit a uniform randomized $k$-server implementation with that ratio, polynomial preprocessing and per-request bit cost, and a finite instance-dependent additive allowance.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{KServer.lean}, \texttt{UniformKServer.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.KServer.main\_theorem}, \texttt{OAI.UniformKServer.uniform\_squared\_logarithmic\_main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

